  "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=\u27e8{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2},\\,2t\u27e9,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{\u2032}(t)\\times {r}^{\u2033}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j.$*",
  "worked_solution": "The curvature $\u03ba(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u03ba(t)=\\frac{\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\\times {r}^{\u2033}(t)\u2016}{\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t){\u2016}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and $\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\u2016:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82t,\\,4t,\\,2\u27e9 \\\\ \u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\u2016 & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(4t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(5{t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{\u2032}(t)\\times {r}^{\u2033}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\\times {r}^{\u2033}(t)\u2016 & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03ba(t) & =\\frac{\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\\times {r}^{\u2033}(t)\u2016}{\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t){\u2016}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{(2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{8\\sqrt{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
